  "comments": [
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "18391007"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "19477185"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "20582320"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Major outer membrane lipoprotein present exclusively in pathogenic Leptospira species (PubMed:18391007). It displays high immunogenicity (PubMed:18391007). Binds in vitro to extracellular matrix (ECM) proteins such as collagen type IV (PubMed:18391007). It also binds host blood proteins such as plasma fibronectin and plasminogen (PubMed:18391007, PubMed:20582320). It specifically binds calcium ions (PubMed:19477185)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "FUNCTION"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "18391007"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "19477185"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Binds Ca(2+) ions, which induces conformational changes and increases the thermal stability of the protein (PubMed:19477185). Binding is specific for Ca(2+) as no structural or stability perturbations are observed for Mg(2+), Zn(2+) or Cu(2+) (PubMed:19477185). Heparin and gelatin can inhibit binding to fibronectin in a concentration-dependent manner (PubMed:18391007)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "ACTIVITY REGULATION"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "19255491"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "19477185"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Homodimer"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "SUBUNIT"
    },
    {
      "commentType": "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION",
      "note": {
        "texts": [
          {
            "evidences": [
              {
                "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
                "source": "PubMed",
                "id": "23323152"
              }
            ],
            "value": "Was identified as a surface-exposed outer membrane lipoprotein. However, one study shows that the protein is not detected on the surface of intact cells by immunofluorescence assay (IFA), but becomes accessible to antibodies only after permeabilization of the outer membrane, suggesting that LipL32 is not exposed on the surface, but that it may be a sub-surface membrane lipoprotein on the inner leaflet of the outer membrane"
          }
        ]
      },
      "subcellularLocations": [
        {
          "location": {
            "evidences": [
              {
                "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
                "source": "PubMed",
                "id": "23323152"
              }
            ],
            "value": "Cell outer membrane",
            "id": "SL-0040"
          },
          "topology": {
            "evidences": [
              {
                "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000255",
                "source": "PROSITE-ProRule",
                "id": "PRU00303"
              }
            ],
            "value": "Lipid-anchor",
            "id": "SL-9901"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "18391007"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "19477185"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Possesses a jelly-roll fold core from which several peripheral secondary structures protrude (PubMed:19477185). The C-terminus is the primary immunogenic domain, it is recognized early in the course of infection and mediates interaction with collagen IV and plasma fibronectin (PubMed:18391007)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "DOMAIN"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "25356675"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Undergoes post-translational modifications, particularly acetylation and tri-methylation of lysine residues, under in vivo growth conditions (PubMed:25356675). In comparison, Leptospira grown in culture does not result in any LipL32 lysine modifications (PubMed:25356675). Modifications may play a role in regulating protein architecture and function, and alter protein recognition by the host immune response (PubMed:25356675)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "PTM"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "37426019"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Silencing of the gene results in more virulent Leptospira cells, culminating in early and more severe symptom presentation in hamsters, accompanied by higher liver and kidney loads (PubMed:37426019). The interaction of the mutant with host components is unaffected, except for a slight increase in plasminogen binding (PubMed:37426019)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "DISRUPTION PHENOTYPE"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "18391007"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "33137154"
            }
          ],
          "value": "The C-terminal and intermediate domains of LipL32 are recognized by sera of patients with laboratory-confirmed leptospirosis, suggesting that it could be used as a diagnostic antigen for the acute and convalescent phases of patients with leptospirosis (PubMed:18391007). A real-time PCR with high specificity, which has the capability to detect all known Leptospira pathogenic species, was developed based on the lipL32 gene (PubMed:33137154)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "BIOTECHNOLOGY"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "23206414"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "31931114"
            }
          ],
          "value": "The sequence conservation and immunogenicity of LipL32 make the protein an attractive target for vaccine design (PubMed:23206414). However, despite the immunogenicity of LipL32, the efficacy of LipL32 vaccines is unconvincing, with either no protection or low levels of protection observed in the majority of studies (PubMed:23206414). Due to these limitations, it is important to develop novel immunization strategies, such as antibody therapy, which can provide immediate and effective protection against bacterial infections (PubMed:31931114). Anti-LipL32 monoclonal antibodies are effective in the early stages of infection and can reduce disease severity in an animal model (PubMed:31931114)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "BIOTECHNOLOGY"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "19606093"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Is the most abundant protein in Leptospira with an estimated 38'000 copies per cell"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "MISCELLANEOUS"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Belongs to the LipL32 family"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "SIMILARITY"
    }
  ],
